<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trans-Siberian Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1891–1904</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trans-Siberian Railway is the railway line linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-russia">European Russia</a> with the Pacific coast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladivostok">Vladivostok</a>, the longest in the world. Construction began in 1891, and the line was largely in operation by 1904. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> profile treats it as a major infrastructure project that integrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and the Far East into the imperial economy and supported Russian expansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway.jpg" alt="Trans-Siberian Railway" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sorovas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VL_85-022_container_train.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
